course: space
lesson: 28
title: How we know: measuring the sky
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Nobody has flown to a star with a tape measure, yet we know how far stars are and what they are made of: astronomers are detectives who read clues in light.
explain: Hold a finger at arm's length and wink one eye, then the other: the finger jumps against the wall. Stars do the same as Earth moves round the Sun, and the smaller the jump, the farther the star. This is parallax.
explain: Split starlight into a rainbow with a prism and you see thin dark lines, like a barcode: each element (a basic kind of stuff, like hydrogen, helium or iron) leaves its own lines, so we can tell what a star is made of.
explain: Light from a galaxy rushing away is stretched towards red, like a siren dropping its pitch as an ambulance drives off: the bigger the stretch, the faster it goes.
explain: Some answers came first from pure reasoning: Neptune was found in 1846 exactly where maths said a hidden planet must be pulling Uranus.
fact: Astronomers measure distances to nearby stars by parallax: a star seems to shift slightly against far-off stars as Earth moves round the Sun; the farther apart the two places you look from, the bigger the shift.
fact: In 1838 Friedrich Bessel measured the parallax of the star 61 Cygni, the first star distance ever measured.
fact: The European Space Agency's Gaia spacecraft measured the positions and motions of nearly two billion stars before it was switched off in 2025.
fact: Around 240 BC Eratosthenes in Egypt estimated the size of Earth by comparing the shadows of the Sun at two cities: because Earth's surface is curved, the noon Sun stood straight overhead at one and cast a shadow at the other.
fact: Splitting starlight into a rainbow, called a spectrum, shows dark lines that reveal which elements the star is made of.
fact: Helium was discovered in the Sun's spectrum in 1868, more than 25 years before it was found on Earth, and its name comes from the Greek word for Sun.
fact: In 1925 Cecilia Payne showed from star spectra that stars are made mostly of hydrogen and helium, a finding other astronomers at first refused to accept.
fact: Light from a galaxy moving away from us is stretched to redder colours, called redshift, and the amount tells how fast it is moving.
fact: Henrietta Leavitt found in 1912 that certain pulsing stars called Cepheids pulse more slowly the brighter they really are, letting astronomers measure great distances: if a Cepheid looks dimmer than its pulse says it really is, it must be farther away.
fact: Neptune was found in 1846 where Urbain Le Verrier predicted it should be, from the way Uranus was being pulled off course.
fact: Astronomers can weigh the Sun by using how fast Earth goes round it and how far away it is: the heavier the Sun, the harder it pulls and the faster Earth must go round.
exercise: What is parallax? | A star's small apparent shift as Earth moves round the Sun | The twinkling of stars in the air | A star's slow change of colour as it grows older and cooler | The bending of light round the Sun
exercise: The smaller a star's parallax, the... | farther away the star is | closer the star is | hotter the star is | younger the star is
exercise: Which star's distance was measured first, in 1838, and by whom? | 61 Cygni, by Friedrich Bessel | Polaris, by Galileo | Sirius, by Newton | Proxima Centauri, by Hubble
exercise: What did the Gaia spacecraft do? | Measured the positions and motions of nearly two billion stars | Landed on the Sun | Took the very first picture of a black hole's shadow in a far galaxy | Took people to the Moon
exercise: How did Eratosthenes estimate the size of Earth around 240 BC? | By comparing the Sun's shadows at two cities | By sailing all the way round it | By measuring it from space | By counting the steps people took walking from Egypt to China
exercise: What is a spectrum of starlight? | Starlight spread into a rainbow of colours | A close-up photo of the star's bright surface | The low humming sound a star makes as it burns | The curved path a star takes across the night sky
exercise: What do the dark lines in a star's spectrum reveal? | Which elements the star is made of | How old the people who first saw it were | Where the star's planets are hiding in its glare | Tiny cracks and scratches in the telescope's lens
exercise: Where was helium first discovered? | In the Sun's spectrum in 1868 | In a balloon factory | In rocks from the Moon | In the air of Mars
exercise: Where does the name helium come from? | The Greek word for Sun | The Latin word for balloon | The name of its finder | The German word for light
exercise: What did Cecilia Payne show in 1925? | Stars are made mostly of hydrogen and helium | Stars are made mostly of iron, like the Earth's core | The Sun is made of burning coal, like a huge fire | Stars are just as old as the Earth, and no older
exercise: What is redshift? | Light from a receding galaxy stretched to redder colours | Stars slowly turning red as they cool down during the night | The red glow of Mars | A red filter on telescopes
exercise: What did Henrietta Leavitt discover about Cepheid stars? | The slower they pulse, the brighter they really are | They pulse faster when they are older | They never change in brightness | They are all exactly the same distance away from the Earth
exercise: How was Neptune found in 1846? | Where Le Verrier's maths predicted, from Uranus being pulled | By accident, when a child looked through a small home telescope | By a spacecraft flying past | It was always visible to the naked eye
exercise: How can astronomers weigh the Sun? | From how fast Earth goes round it and how far away it is | By putting a piece of it on scales | By measuring how hot it feels on a summer day at different places | By counting its sunspots
test: Lily thinks astronomers must fly to stars to know what they're made of. What is right? | The lines in their light show what they are made of | She is right, probes have visited all of them and dug up samples | Nobody knows what stars are made of, it is still a total mystery | They guess from the star's colour only, nothing else helps
test: Hold a pencil close to your face, then far, and wink each eye. When does it jump less? | When it is far away, just like distant stars | When it is close | It jumps the same both times | It never jumps at all
test: Why would it be easier to measure parallax from a planet with a much bigger orbit than Earth? | Looking from two places farther apart makes the shift bigger | Stars are closer to the bigger orbits, so they look larger | The air is much clearer for planets with big orbits | Stars stand still for bigger planets, so they are easier to aim at
test: An ambulance's siren drops in pitch as it drives away. Which sky clue works like that? | Redshift of a galaxy moving away | Parallax of a nearby star | Dark lines of helium | The phases of Venus
test: Why was it so amazing that helium was found in the Sun before on Earth? | Scientists identified a new element from 150 million km away | Helium is the heaviest element | The Sun is made only of helium | It proved that the Sun is really cold underneath its glowing outside
test: Two Cepheids pulse equally slowly, but one looks much dimmer. What can you conclude? | The dimmer one is much farther away | The dimmer one is much nearer | They must be the same distance | The dimmer one is younger
test: Payne's result was first refused by other astronomers. What does her story show about science? | Good evidence can win even when experts disagree at first | Whatever famous experts say is always right in the end, every time | Young scientists are always wrong | Once rejected, an idea is never accepted
test: Uranus kept drifting off its predicted path. What did Le Verrier reason? | An unseen planet's gravity must be pulling it | Uranus must be running out of fuel and slowing down | The telescopes must all be broken and showing it wrong | Uranus must be changing its mass as it loses its rings
test: If Earth went round the Sun faster at the same distance, what would that tell us about the Sun? | It would be heavier than we think | It would be lighter than we think | It would be colder | Nothing at all
test: Eratosthenes saw no shadow at noon in one city but a shadow at another the same day. Why? | Earth's surface is curved, so the Sun hits at different angles | One city had taller buildings | The Sun was in two places at once | The other city was much nearer the Moon, which blocked the sunlight
test: bro reckons nobody can know how far stars are, it's all made up. true? | No, parallax measures nearby stars directly, like Gaia did | Yes, star distances are pure guesses | Yes, nobody has ever tried | No, astronauts have measured every one of them with long lasers
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the method of stellar parallax? | The apparent shift of a nearby star as the Earth orbits the Sun | The variation in colour of a star with its temperature | The pulsation of certain variable stars | The stretching of light from galaxies which are receding from the Earth
exam: Identify the element first discovered in the spectrum of the Sun in 1868. | Helium | Hydrogen | Oxygen | Iron
exam: Ascertain the means by which the planet Neptune was located in 1846. | Prediction from the disturbed motion of Uranus | Observation by a passing spacecraft | Chance observation with the naked eye | Detection of its radio emissions
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the composition of stars as shown by Cecilia Payne in 1925. | Stars consist mostly of hydrogen and helium | Stars consist mostly of iron and nickel | Stars consist mostly of carbon and oxygen | Stars have the same composition as the Earth
